## Coding Standards

These standards are managed by Cerebro and apply to every repository you
work in. The intent is to capture decisions you have already made about
how you want an agent to work — branching, commits, PRs, testing, prose
and code style — so each new conversation starts from the same baseline
without having to relitigate the basics.

### Branching

{% if branching.work_on_main_allowed -%}
- Working directly on the main branch is allowed when a change is small
  and obviously safe (typo fix, log message). For anything else, branch.
{%- else -%}
- Never commit directly to the main branch. Always work on a feature
  branch and open a pull request.
{%- endif %}
{% if branching.ask_before_creating -%}
- Ask before creating a new branch. Confirm the branch name and the
  scope of the change before checking out.
{%- else -%}
- Create branches as needed without asking. The branch name should
  describe the work, not the ticket.
{%- endif %}
- Branch naming scheme: {{ branching.naming_scheme }}.

### Commits

{% if commits.descriptive_messages -%}
- Commit messages describe *why* the change exists, not just *what*
  changed. The diff already shows what; the message exists for the
  reader who needs to understand the motivation.
{%- else -%}
- Commit messages can be terse. Optimize for getting work committed.
{%- endif %}
{% if commits.conventional -%}
- Use conventional commit prefixes (`feat:`, `fix:`, `chore:`, `docs:`,
  `refactor:`, `test:`). The prefix sets the tone for the rest of the
  message.
{%- else -%}
- Conventional commit prefixes are not required. Use plain prose.
{%- endif %}
{% if commits.signed -%}
- All commits must be GPG-signed. Do not bypass signing with
  `--no-gpg-sign` for any reason.
{%- else -%}
- Commit signing is not required.
{%- endif %}

### Pull requests

{% if prs.tests_must_pass -%}
- All tests must pass locally before opening a pull request. Do not
  open a PR with known-failing tests and a "will fix in CI" note.
{%- else -%}
- Tests do not have to pass locally before opening a PR; CI is the
  source of truth.
{%- endif %}
{% if prs.description_template -%}
- PR descriptions follow the template: a "Summary" section
  (1-3 bullet points on what changed and why) and a "Test plan"
  section (a checklist of how the change was verified).
{%- else -%}
- PR description format is freeform.
{%- endif %}

### Testing

{% if testing.tdd -%}
- Practice test-driven development. Write the failing test first, then
  the smallest production change that makes it pass.
{%- else -%}
- TDD is not required. Tests can follow the implementation.
{%- endif %}
- Minimum coverage expectation for new code: {{ testing.min_coverage }}.

### Prose style (in code, comments, docs, commit messages)

- No emdashes. If you reach for an emdash, restructure the sentence.
- Sentence fragments are fine when they read naturally.
- Be terse but descriptive. Avoid hedging language ("perhaps",
  "it might be the case that"). State things directly.

### Code style

- Names matter more than comments. A well-named function or variable
  removes the need for a comment that explains *what* it does.
- Comments exist to explain *why*. The code already shows what.
- Prefer obvious code over clever code. The reader's time is more
  valuable than the writer's typing time.
